-- Q2400: Circular 230 [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under Circular 230 Section 10.50(c), censure is a public reprimand expressing disapproval of the practitioner''s conduct. It is the least severe of the three primary disciplinary sanctions and does not restrict or terminate the practitioner''s right to practice before the IRS.
Wrong (B): A temporary prohibition from practice describes suspension under Section 10.50(a), which is more severe than censure.
Wrong (C): Circular 230 does not provide for monetary penalties equal to the client''s deficiency as a disciplinary sanction against practitioners.
Wrong (D): A permanent prohibition from practice describes disbarment under Section 10.50(a), which is the most severe sanction.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2400;

-- Q2401: Circular 230 [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under Circular 230 Section 10.23, a practitioner with a valid power of attorney must exercise due diligence by promptly informing the client of IRS correspondence and taking appropriate action within the scope of the engagement. This ensures the client''s interests and deadlines are protected.
Wrong (A): Responding without informing the client violates the practitioner''s duty to keep the client informed under Section 10.23.
Wrong (B): A practitioner cannot simply ignore IRS notices. Due diligence obligations apply regardless of the tax year covered by the power of attorney.
Wrong (D): Returning the notice and requesting direct client correspondence abandons the practitioner''s responsibilities under the power of attorney.$EXPL$
