-- Q2402: Circular 230 [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under Circular 230 Section 10.29, a practitioner may represent conflicting interests before the IRS if each party gives informed written consent after full disclosure of the conflict. The practitioner must also reasonably believe competent and diligent representation can be provided to each client.
Wrong (A): Dual representation is not always prohibited. Section 10.29 permits it when informed written consent is obtained from all parties.
Wrong (B): Spouses are not treated as one taxpayer for conflict purposes. Each spouse is a separate client whose individual interests must be evaluated.
Wrong (C): Referral to separate practitioners is not mandatory. The practitioner may continue if proper consent is obtained under Section 10.29.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2402;

-- Q2403: Professional Ethics in Tax [easy/L4]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AICPA SSTS No. 1 (consistent with IRC Sec. 6694 preparer penalty thresholds), a CPA should not recommend a tax return position unless it has at least a realistic possibility of being sustained on its merits. The standard aligns with substantial authority for undisclosed positions and reasonable basis for disclosed positions.
Wrong (B): SSTS No. 1 does not require certainty that a position will be sustained. The realistic possibility standard is a lower threshold than definite success.
Wrong (C): A CPA may not recommend a position solely because the client wishes it. There must be adequate legal support under applicable standards.
Wrong (D): Advance IRS approval is not required. The CPA must independently evaluate the position against applicable tax authority.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2403;

-- Q2404: Professional Ethics in Tax [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AICPA SSTS No. 1, the realistic possibility standard requires approximately a one-in-three (33%) likelihood that a tax return position will be sustained on its merits if challenged. This establishes the minimum confidence level before a CPA may recommend a position without disclosure.
Wrong (A): A greater than 50% chance describes the more-likely-than-not standard under IRC Sec. 6662(d)(2)(B) for avoiding substantial understatement penalties, which is a higher threshold than realistic possibility.
Wrong (C): No SSTS provision requires specific published IRS guidance authorizing a particular position. The CPA evaluates all applicable tax authority.
Wrong (D): A private letter ruling is not required. The realistic possibility standard is met through analysis of existing tax law, not IRS-specific approval.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2404;

-- Q2405: Professional Ethics in Tax [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under Circular 230 Section 10.21, when a practitioner discovers client noncompliance, the practitioner must promptly advise the client of the noncompliance, its consequences, and available corrective steps. The practitioner is not required to report the client to the IRS.
Wrong (A): Circular 230 does not require practitioners to notify IRS Criminal Investigation. The disclosure obligation runs to the client, not to the government.
Wrong (B): The practitioner is not required to terminate the engagement immediately. The obligation is to advise the client and recommend corrective action.
Wrong (C): The practitioner has no authority to file corrective returns on the client''s behalf without authorization. The decision to amend belongs to the client.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2405;

