-- Q2407: Professional Ethics in Tax [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AICPA SSTS No. 4, a CPA may use estimates on a tax return when it is impracticable to obtain exact data and the estimates are reasonable based on facts and circumstances known to the CPA. The CPA should not present estimates as exact figures.
Wrong (B): SSTS No. 4 does not require specific IRS guidance authorizing estimates for a particular item. The reasonableness standard is based on the CPA''s professional judgment.
Wrong (C): No dollar threshold or materiality limit applies. SSTS No. 4 permits estimates whenever exact data is impracticable to obtain, regardless of amount.
Wrong (D): SSTS No. 4 expressly permits estimates when exact figures are unavailable. Requiring exact figures in all cases would be impractical.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2407;

-- Q2409: Professional Ethics in Tax [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AICPA SSTS No. 2, a CPA may leave a question unanswered when the information is not readily available and the answer is not significant in determining the correct tax liability. The CPA must consider whether the omission may cause the return to be deemed incomplete.
Wrong (A): A CPA may not leave any question unanswered without limitation. SSTS No. 2 requires the CPA to evaluate the significance of each omitted answer individually.
Wrong (C): A written client directive is not the governing standard. The CPA must independently assess whether the omission is appropriate under SSTS No. 2.
Wrong (D): SSTS No. 2 does not require every question to be answered. It permits omissions when the information is unavailable and the answer is not significant.$EXPL$
