-- Q2411: Professional Ethics in Tax [medium/L4]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AICPA SSTS No. 5, a CPA may recommend a position contrary to a prior IRS administrative settlement when facts and circumstances have changed or new legal authority supports a different position. Prior determinations are not permanent constraints when underlying conditions differ.
Wrong (A): The passage of time alone does not justify departing from a prior settlement. SSTS No. 5 requires an objective basis such as changed facts or new authority.
Wrong (B): Client insistence without supporting authority is insufficient. The CPA must independently evaluate whether changed facts or new authority justify the departure.
Wrong (D): The likelihood of future audit is irrelevant. SSTS No. 5 focuses on whether substantive conditions have changed, not enforcement probability.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2411;

-- Q2412: Professional Ethics in Tax [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AICPA SSTS No. 1, a CPA should not recommend a tax return position unless the CPA has a good-faith belief that the position has a realistic possibility of being sustained on its merits if challenged. This is generally interpreted as approximately a one-in-three likelihood.
Wrong (A): Certainty is not required. The realistic possibility standard is a lower threshold that requires only a reasonable level of support, not guaranteed success.
Wrong (B): A private letter ruling is not required before recommending a position. The CPA evaluates the position against all applicable tax authority.
Wrong (C): A 75% probability exceeds the realistic possibility standard. SSTS No. 1 requires approximately 33%, not 75%.$EXPL$
