-- Q2414: Professional Ethics in Tax [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AICPA SSTS No. 6, when a CPA becomes aware of a possible error on a previously filed return — regardless of who prepared it — the CPA should promptly inform the client and recommend corrective measures such as filing an amended return.
Wrong (A): The CPA may not ignore the error simply because another firm prepared the prior return. SSTS No. 6 applies whenever the CPA becomes aware of a possible error.
Wrong (B): The CPA should not contact the prior firm without the client''s consent. The obligation runs to the client, not to the predecessor preparer.
Wrong (D): Reporting to the IRS whistleblower office is not required. SSTS No. 6 directs the CPA to inform the client, not to report errors to the government.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2414;

-- Q2416: Professional Ethics in Tax [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under the AICPA Code of Professional Conduct, CPAs performing tax services must maintain integrity and objectivity. Unlike attest engagements, tax engagements do not require independence from the client, but the CPA must not subordinate professional judgment to client wishes.
Wrong (A): Independence identical to attest engagements is not required for tax services. The AICPA independence rules apply primarily to audit and review engagements.
Wrong (B): CPAs have ethical obligations beyond IRS requirements. The AICPA Code of Professional Conduct imposes standards of integrity and objectivity for all services.
Wrong (C): Client loyalty does not override all other obligations. The CPA must maintain objectivity and cannot subordinate professional judgment to the client.$EXPL$
