-- Q2418: Professional Ethics in Tax [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under Circular 230 Section 10.21 and AICPA SSTS No. 6, when a CPA discovers a client used fraudulent information on a return, the CPA must withdraw from the engagement and advise the client to take corrective action. The CPA must not sign future returns affected by the fraud.
Wrong (A): Continuing the engagement is not permissible once fraud is discovered, even if the CPA was unaware at the time of filing. Knowledge of fraud creates an immediate obligation to act.
Wrong (B): Circular 230 does not require reporting the client to law enforcement absent a court order. The CPA''s obligation is to withdraw, not to report.
Wrong (D): Filing an amended return without client consent exceeds the CPA''s authority. The CPA advises corrective action but cannot unilaterally amend the return.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2418;

-- Q2419: Professional Ethics in Tax [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AICPA SSTS No. 3, when a discrepancy exists between a third-party information return and the client''s claim, the CPA must make reasonable inquiries, request supporting documentation, and exercise professional judgment to determine the correct amount to report.
Wrong (A): Blindly accepting the 1099 amount without inquiry ignores the CPA''s duty to evaluate the accuracy of information. SSTS No. 3 requires investigation of discrepancies.
Wrong (B): Splitting the difference has no basis in professional standards. The CPA must determine the correct amount through inquiry and documentation review.
Wrong (C): Accepting the client''s oral statement alone without supporting documentation is insufficient. SSTS No. 3 requires the CPA to evaluate the credibility of the client''s position.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2419;

-- Q2421: Professional Ethics in Tax [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): The AICPA independence rules apply primarily to attest engagements such as audits and reviews. A CPA may perform tax preparation and bookkeeping services for the same client without violating independence rules, as these are non-attest services.
Wrong (A): Performing both tax and bookkeeping services does not violate independence rules. Independence requirements apply to attest engagements, not non-attest services.
Wrong (B): No waiver from the state board is required. Dual non-attest services are permitted under the AICPA Code of Professional Conduct without special authorization.
Wrong (D): Disclosure of the dual relationship to the IRS is not required. The AICPA Code does not mandate IRS notification for providing multiple non-attest services.$EXPL$
