-- Q2430: Tax Procedures [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 6501(c), there is no statute of limitations on assessment when the taxpayer files a false or fraudulent return with intent to evade tax, or when the taxpayer fails to file a return at all. The IRS may assess additional tax at any time in these cases.
Wrong (B): Filing a late return does not eliminate the statute of limitations. A late-filed return starts the three-year assessment period from the actual filing date under Section 6501(a).
Wrong (C): Underpaying estimated taxes does not affect the assessment statute of limitations. It may trigger estimated tax penalties under Section 6654 but does not extend the assessment period.
Wrong (D): Claiming too many exemptions does not eliminate the statute of limitations. This may result in an accuracy-related penalty but does not remove the time limit on assessment.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2430;

-- Q2432: Tax Procedures [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Sec. 7803(e), after receiving a 30-day letter, a taxpayer may request a conference with the IRS Independent Office of Appeals, which provides an independent administrative review with settlement authority. For deficiencies exceeding $25,000, a formal written protest is required.
Wrong (B): The U.S. Supreme Court does not hear initial tax disputes. It only reviews cases on certiorari from lower courts after all other appellate avenues are exhausted.
Wrong (C): Submitting a protest to the examining agent is not the proper procedure. The written protest is directed to the Appeals Office, which is independent of the examination function.
Wrong (D): Congressional representatives do not have authority to intervene in IRS examination proceedings. The administrative appeal process is the proper avenue.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2432;

-- Q2433: Tax Procedures [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under 28 U.S.C. Sec. 1346(a)(1), the U.S. District Court has jurisdiction over federal tax refund suits and is the only court that provides the right to a jury trial in tax disputes. The taxpayer must first pay the assessed tax and file a refund claim under IRC Sec. 7422(a).
Wrong (A): The U.S. Tax Court adjudicates deficiency cases before judges without juries. It does not provide jury trials.
Wrong (B): The U.S. Court of Federal Claims under 28 U.S.C. Sec. 1491 uses bench trials only. It does not offer jury trials in tax cases.
Wrong (D): The IRS Appeals Office is an administrative body, not a court. It cannot conduct jury trials or issue binding judicial decisions.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2433;

-- Q2434: Tax Procedures [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 6651(a)(1), the failure-to-file penalty is 5% of the unpaid tax for each month or part of a month the return is late, up to a maximum of 25%. This penalty is separate from and in addition to the failure-to-pay penalty.
Wrong (B): The 0.5% rate applies to the failure-to-pay penalty under Section 6651(a)(2), not the failure-to-file penalty. The failure-to-file rate is ten times higher at 5%.
Wrong (C): There is no flat $500 penalty for failure to file. The penalty is calculated as a percentage of the unpaid tax balance, not as a fixed dollar amount.
Wrong (D): The penalty is not 10% of total tax liability. It is 5% per month of the unpaid tax, capped at 25%.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2434;

-- Q2435: Tax Procedures [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Section 6651(a)(2), the failure-to-pay penalty is 0.5% of the unpaid tax per month or part of a month, up to 25%. Filing the return on time avoids the more severe failure-to-file penalty but does not eliminate the failure-to-pay penalty.
Wrong (A): The 5% rate applies to the failure-to-file penalty under Section 6651(a)(1), not the failure-to-pay penalty. The failure-to-pay rate is much lower at 0.5%.
Wrong (B): There is no flat 10% penalty for failure to pay. The penalty is calculated monthly as a percentage of the unpaid balance.
Wrong (C): Filing on time does not eliminate all penalties. The failure-to-pay penalty under Section 6651(a)(2) applies whenever the tax shown on the return remains unpaid.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2435;

